{
  "teamId": 139,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 Tampa Bay Rays opened their season with a spark that hinted at something special. Kameron Misner's Opening Day home run set an optimistic tone, and through late April the Rays rattled off a string of clutch performances — Brandon Lowe's two-run single, Yandy Díaz coming through in back-to-back games, and Eric Orze inducing a momentum-swinging double play — that suggested this team had the fight to compete in a tough American League East.\n\nThe cracks began to show in May and deepened through June. Edwin Uceta became a recurring villain in the bullpen's story, surrendering home runs that swung games decisively away from Tampa Bay. The Blue Jays torched the Rays across a two-game stretch in mid-May, with Daulton Varsho's three-run bomb and Alejandro Kirk's crushing shot doing lasting damage. By early June, errors and late-inning collapses against Boston compounded the misery, and what had looked like a competitive roster began to feel brittle.\n\nJuly brought little relief. Kevin Kelly surrendered back-to-back damaging home runs over the Fourth of July weekend against Minnesota, and Pete Fairbanks — the veteran closer — became a liability, giving up two soul-crushing blasts in the same week. The worst moment of the season arrived on July 11, when Ceddanne Rafaela's two-run homer off Fairbanks cost Tampa Bay a -0.821 swing in win probability, the single most damaging play of the year. The Rays were in freefall.\n\nThe final stretch offered flashes of resilience — Josh Lowe's two-run homer on July 30, Hunter Feduccia's clutch double in August, and Nick Fortes going deep in September — but the bullpen continued to hemorrhage leads. Kevin Kelly surrendered two more home runs on the same late-September afternoon as the season wound down. The Rays finished 77-85, their pitching staff's 3.94 ERA masking a bullpen that simply could not hold games in the balance. A promising offense that slugged 182 home runs and a rotation with genuine depth were ultimately undermined by a late-game relief corps that turned too many winnable contests into losses, leaving Tampa Bay on the outside of the playoff picture looking in.",
  "chapters": [
